r he's collecting the coin  for himself instead  like the first season the second  season's episode logos are easter eggs  to significant characters and moments in  the show the winged vampire symbol for  the first episode references the brook  sir verena who lives with nivalin the  vines growing out of the wolf's head in  the second episode's logo foreshadow  eskal's fate and how the leshi monster  will end up growing inside him the  swallow in episode 3 represents siri  whose name derives from the elder word  for swallow the sixth episode hand with  fingers that turn into flames is a  reference to the mage reince who can  conjure fire from his bare finger  interestingly the way he gets his facial  burns in the show when jennifer blows  alcohol in his face while he's holding  up a fiery finger is a nod to how she  burned his face in the books by sending  a fire spell through a closing portal  and riance's partner in crime lydia gets  her facial disfigurement after  attempting to blood trace the stolen  vial of siri's elder blood which is a  hat tip to how she was similarly injured  in the books after experimenting on a  mysterious cursed object found in an  acropolis  the final episode's logo of spheres  colliding and smashing into shards which  reform into a tree and not only a  reference to the conjunction of the  spheres but also a hint to the reveal of  a hidden monolith inside the witcher  medallion tree and how siri will split  the tree in two then shatter the stone  to pieces later on in the episode and  there's a clue early in the season of  siri's connection to season two's  monsters when geralt finds that branches  from the lashi and kermoren have entered  siri's room and pulled the shawl of her  dead grandmother kelanthe into the walls  there was a clue to the deathless mother  bringing basilisks into this world when  fringilla talked about the scary story  she heard as a child stories about the  witch in the woods her home perched on  basilisk legs the 